      "text": "Testing a browser game requires a comprehensive approach that goes beyond simple visual inspection, as web-based games rely heavily on client-side JavaScript and browser rendering engines. To ensure a robust and enjoyable user experience, developers must systematically test several critical components, starting with keyboard input. This involves verifying that every key press triggers the intended action without delay or error. Testers should check for key repeat issues, ensuring that holding down a movement key results in smooth, continuous motion rather than stuttering. Additionally, it is crucial to test for key conflicts, ensuring that simultaneous presses of multiple keys do not cause the game to freeze or ignore inputs unexpectedly.\n\nCollision detection is another vital area that demands rigorous testing. Developers must verify that hitboxes align accurately with the visual sprites of characters and obstacles. Testing should include edge cases, such as objects grazing each other or overlapping partially, to ensure the collision logic is precise. It is also important to test collision responses, such as bouncing off walls or triggering damage when hitting an enemy, to confirm that the game logic reacts correctly to these interactions.\n\nScoring systems must be validated to ensure points are awarded accurately and consistently. Testers should check for potential exploits, such as repeatedly triggering a scoring event without resetting the state, which could lead to inflated scores. Furthermore"
